A43 — report an X11-drawn game window to the daemon

The daemon clears the app's booting splash when it receives a GameWindowNote on
the scan-out channel. Only the guest ICD shim sends one, from its swapchain
present path, so a game that never presents through the shim's WSI proxy keeps
the splash up while it runs behind it: an OpenGL game whose zink output reaches
X as plain drawing (Balatro), or a GDI game.

Such a game draws into a child of wine's desktop window, and Xwayland commits
the desktop window's own surface for every frame. So while no override surface
is bound on the focused window, its main-surface commits are counted per
second; when a second carries at least 20 of them and the desktop window has a
game child (the shim's own rule: viewable, InputOutput, larger than 1x1, a
readable WM_CLASS instance that differs from the desktop's), the compositor
sends the note itself on a fresh GAMENATIVE_DRM_SOCKET connection. At most three
notes are sent, one per qualifying second, so a note dropped by a racing
connection is sent again; the daemon latches the first.

A game that presents through the shim is not affected: once its override
surface is bound its frames no longer land on the main surface and the count is
reset, and before its first present a mapped, still window commits far below
the threshold, so the splash is not lifted over it early.

--- a/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
+++ b/src/steamcompmgr.cpp
@@ -68,6 +68,7 @@
 #include <sys/procctl.h>
 #endif
 #include <sys/socket.h>
+#include <sys/un.h>
 #include <sys/resource.h>
 #include <time.h>
 #include <unistd.h>
@@ -8710,6 +8711,161 @@ void update_wayland_res(CommitDoneList_t *doneCommits, steamcompmgr_win_t *w, Re
